From 0f1578d407ed16eb14d029e880e5da4207ff8728 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?=C3=98yvind=20Harboe?= <oyvind.harboe@zylin.com>
Date: Mon, 31 Aug 2026 13:08:42 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] fix(flow): don't leak user_stages into orfs_update

orfs_flow() reads user_stages with .get(), so it stays in kwargs.
_orfs_stage() pops it for every stage target, but orfs_update is not a
stage and its rule has no such attribute. The config.mk DSL always
passes it -- orfs_design() defaults user_stages to {} and forwards it
unconditionally -- so every design that has a rules-base.json fails
analysis with:

  no such attribute 'user_stages' in 'orfs_update' rule

_strip_tool_kwargs() only strips openroad/opensta/yosys, so it does not
cover this. Pop user_stages next to the substeps/lint pops already at
the call site.
---
 private/flow.bzl | 8 ++++++++
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+)

diff --git a/private/flow.bzl b/private/flow.bzl
index 3988dff..0bcfe01 100644
--- a/private/flow.bzl
+++ b/private/flow.bzl
@@ -882,6 +882,14 @@ def _orfs_pass(
             update_kwargs = dict(kwargs)
             update_kwargs.pop("substeps", None)
             update_kwargs.pop("lint", None)
+
+            # orfs_flow() reads user_stages with .get(), so it stays in
+            # kwargs; _orfs_stage() pops it for every stage target, but
+            # orfs_update is not a stage and has no such attr. The
+            # config.mk DSL always passes it (orfs_design defaults it to
+            # {}), so without this every design with a RULES_JSON fails
+            # analysis with "no such attribute 'user_stages'".
+            update_kwargs.pop("user_stages", None)
             orfs_update(
                 name = _step_name(name, variant, "update"),
                 rules_json = sources["RULES_JSON"][0],
